window.VA = {"site": {"name": "Elmwood Cemetery", "title": "Elmwood Cemetery Veterans Memorial", "address": "493 Northampton Street, Holyoke, MA", "kind": "cemetery", "center": [42.1869, -72.6291], "zoom": 17, "nameField": "bio"}, "veterans": [{"id": "aaron_f_baldwin_cw", "name": "Aaron F. Baldwin", "era": "Civil War", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187094, "lng": -72.629106, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/baldwin.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "The smoke of the American Civil War was still settling when Aaron F. Baldwin answered the call to defend the Union. He donned the iconic blue uniform and stepped into the ranks of Company F, 27th Massachusetts Volunteer Infantry, a regiment destined for some of the conflict's most grueling campaigns. Baldwin's service pulled him straight into the crucible of the war's eastern and southern theaters. With the 27th Massachusetts, he marched through the suffocating swamps of North Carolina and fought under the heavy, musketry-filled skies of Virginia. He endured the relentless rhythm of camp life, the sharp terror of active combat, and the shared resolve of a volunteer regiment pushing deep into enemy territory. Against the odds of a brutal war, Baldwin survived the battlefields and returned home to the rolling hills of the Pioneer Valley and the mills of Holyoke. Today, his long march is over. He rests in eternal peace alongside his loved ones beneath the quiet, shaded canopy of Elmwood Cemetery, his legacy etched into the very soil of the city he helped protect. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "carlton_r_baush_wwi", "name": "Carlton R. Baush", "era": "World War I", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187072, "lng": -72.629203, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/c_baush.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Carlton R. Baush served as a Sergeant in Company C of the 321st Machine Gun Battalion during World War I. The 321st Machine Gun Battalion was assigned to the 164th Infantry Brigade of the 82nd Division — the All-American Division, so named because its draftees came from all 48 states. The 82nd deployed to France in 1918 and fought in the Lorraine campaign, the Battle of St. Mihiel, and the Meuse-Argonne Offensive — one of the largest military operations in American history. Machine gun battalions provided the sustained defensive fire that anchored infantry positions on the Western Front. Baush was born February 20, 1896 and died May 30, 1965. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "lo_carpenter_cw", "name": "Carpenter, Charles O.", "era": "Civil War", "branch": "U.S. Navy • U.S. Army", "branchLabel": "U.S. Navy • U.S. Army", "lat": 42.187439, "lng": -72.629928, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/carpenter.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Born around 1835, Charles O. Carpenter—frequently recorded in early Holyoke registries as L.O. Carpenter—was a trained physician who dedicated his medical skills to the Union cause on both land and sea. He entered the conflict early, commissioning as an Assistant Surgeon in the United States Navy in July 1861. Assigned to the steam gunboat USS Ottawa, Carpenter endured the damp isolation of the South Atlantic Blockading Squadron, treating sailors and managing casualties during coastal bombardments against Confederate fortifications. Resigning his naval commission in March 1863, the continuing pull of the war brought him back to the service in May 1864, this time as a First Lieutenant and Assistant Surgeon with the 57th Massachusetts Volunteer Infantry. Carpenter arrived at the front lines during the absolute climax of the land war, stepping directly into General Grant's bloody Overland Campaign. Working in horrific conditions just behind the firing lines, his field hospitals became places of desperate triage during the slaughters of the Wilderness, Spotsylvania, and Cold Harbor. Covered in blood and breathing in black powder smoke, he performed rapid battlefield amputations under crude anesthesia and fought the rampant outbreaks of typhoid and dysentery that swept through the trenches. He followed the regiment into the grueling Siege of Petersburg before stepping down in January 1865. Following the war, Dr. Carpenter brought his medical practice to the growing community of Holyoke, living out his days as a pillar of local veteran circles. He rests at Elmwood Cemetery, where his grave is adorned with a Grand Army of the Republic marker. Geolocation of his exact plot is pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "patrick_j_curran_wwi", "name": "Patrick J. Curran", "era": "World War I", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.186917, "lng": -72.629203, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/curren.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Born in Holyoke on March 12, 1892, Patrick J. Curran grew up in a rapidly expanding Paper City defined by its bustling canal system and vibrant immigrant neighborhoods. When America mobilized for the First World War, he left his civilian routine to join the United States Army, finding himself on the absolute cutting edge of a military revolution. Sergeant Patrick Curran was assigned to Company C of the 336th Battalion, Tank Corps—a daring new branch established by the American Expeditionary Forces to break the bloody deadlock of trench warfare using mechanized armor. Sergeant Curran and his comrades trained intensely in the infancy of American armored doctrine, a program guided by aggressive young visionaries like George S. Patton. They learned to command and maintain the massive iron beasts, preparing their minds and machinery for a massive upcoming offensive. However, the gears of history moved faster than the tanks could roll into the theater; the Armistice was signed on November 11, 1918, silencing the guns of Europe just as Curran's battalion was prepared to cross the Atlantic. Out of twenty-nine tank battalions raised, only four tasted combat, leaving Curran's unit highly disciplined but waiting in the wings when peace arrived. The Tank Corps was disbanded in 1920, and Patrick returned home to a long civilian life in Holyoke, passing away on December 5, 1965 at the age of 73. He was laid to rest at Elmwood Cemetery. His exact grave site is pending geolocation. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "edward_day_rev", "name": "Day, Edward", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Massachusetts Militia", "branchLabel": "Massachusetts Militia", "lat": 42.187144, "lng": -72.629197, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/day.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Born in West Springfield around 1758, Edward Day was the son of Joel and Eunice Day. He grew up working his family's farm in the Third Parish, coming of age just as the fires of the American Revolution swept through the Pioneer Valley. His household gave an extraordinary measure of devotion to the cause, sending a father and five sons to the front lines. Tragically, Edward was one of the two boys who would never return to the family hearth. In the autumn of 1777, nineteen-year-old Edward marched north with local militia forces to support the crucial Northern Campaign against the British invasion. While thousands of young patriots faced enemy steel in the wilderness around Saratoga, Edward met a much more insidious killer. He contracted the devastating scourge of smallpox and died far from home in Troy, New York, on October 25, 1777, just days after the monumental American victory. To prevent the spread of contagion, he was buried hastily in an unmarked wartime grave near the trail. Today, his name is preserved at Elmwood Cemetery as a cenotaph etched into his father's brownstone headstone, bearing the poignant inscription: \"Edward died in his Country's Service 25th Oct 1777, Æt. 19\". While a 1929 survey charts this memorial stone in the thirteenth row pending geolocation, no grave belonging to Edward himself rests beneath the Holyoke soil. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "robert_day_rev", "name": "Day, Robert", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Patriot Logistics", "branchLabel": "Patriot Logistics", "lat": 42.187164, "lng": -72.629197, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/day.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Born in West Springfield on November 14, 1763, Robert Day was the sixth child born to Joel and Eunice Day. He spent his early childhood on his family's homestead in the Third Parish, growing up in a world where the daily routine was driven entirely by the exhausting labor of clearing the rocky soil and tending crops. When the American Revolution arrived in the valley, his family answered the call with staggering devotion. In the brutal summer of 1777, British General Burgoyne was driving an army south from Canada, and the American forces desperately needed supplies. While his father and five older brothers took up muskets and marched to the front lines, thirteen-year-old Robert volunteered to support the logistical backbone of the campaign. Fort Ticonderoga lay a grueling hundred miles away over rough ox-cart tracks, and the makeshift American army was living hand-to-mouth. Because they lacked an official supply chain, the army survived only because local families sent provisions wagons driven by older men and young boys. Too young to carry a standard-issue weapon into combat, Robert took the reins of a wooden supply wagon to haul food north to the troops. He was not a formal soldier, but he paid the ultimate price for the campaign. The harsh, unsanitary conditions of the trail broke his health, and he succumbed to a fatal military illness on August 27, 1777. His body was never returned to the valley. Today, his memory is preserved at Elmwood Cemetery, though a physical geolocation of an independent grave site is pending; his name is cut directly into his father's brownstone headstone beneath his brother Edward's, bearing the brief, heartbreaking inscription: Robert Day \"died 27 Aug 1777, Aged 13 Years\". If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "william_r_dempsey_wwii", "name": "William R. Dempsey", "era": "World War II", "branch": "Navy", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187283, "lng": -72.628631, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/dempsey.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Born in Holyoke on August 2, 1925, William R. Dempsey grew up navigating the lively urban neighborhoods of a booming, industrial Paper City. He was just a teenager when the global theater of the Second World War pulled America into the fight, transforming his generation overnight. Stepping forward from his working-class neighborhood, William answered the call to serve and donned the uniform of the United States Navy, eventually earning the highly critical rating of Pharmacist's Mate First Class. Navy pharmacist's mates filled one of the most perilous roles in the entire global fleet, serving as frontline medical corpsmen. Whether managing mass casualties in the cramped, shuddering sickbays of warships under heavy artillery fire or sprinting weaponless onto exposed beaches to patch wounds alongside the Marine Corps infantry, these caretakers stood directly in the crossfire to keep men alive. Dempsey survived the harrowing maritime crucible of the war, carrying his memories back to the Pioneer Valley when the guns finally fell silent. Returning to civilian life, he lived a long, full life to the age of 73, passing away on November 23, 1998. Today, he is buried among his fellow local citizens at Elmwood Cemetery, though a definitive physical geolocation of his exact grave site is still pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "hugo_dudman_wwi_wwii", "name": "Hugo Dudman", "era": ["World War I", "World War II"], "branch": "Navy", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187114, "lng": -72.629281, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/dudman.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Born on July 28, 1897, Hugo Dudman spent his youth witnessing the rapid industrial expansion of Holyoke as it grew into a global manufacturing center. Drawn to a life at sea as international tensions flared, he entered the United States Navy during the mobilization for the First World War. He would go on to achieve a rare and venerable distinction in local military history, anchoring a naval career that spanned more than two decades and bridged both global conflicts. Dudman served the fleet as a Signalman Second Class, a vital and highly exposed role before the advent of modern combat radio networks. Positioned high on open, vulnerable signal bridges through the freezing spray of the North Atlantic and the oppressive heat of the Pacific, he was responsible for the visual communications that kept entire armadas coordinated. He translated vital command data into a silent language of flags, semaphores, and flashing blinker lights to navigate fleets through hostile waters. He lived to see the dawn of global peace and the ultimate Allied victory, passing away just months after the close of the second war on April 28, 1946. He was brought home to the Paper City and laid to rest at Elmwood Cemetery, where the physical geolocation of his historic grave site remains pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "elisha_chapin_colonial", "name": "Elisha Chapin", "era": "French & Indian War", "branch": "Provincial Militia", "branchLabel": "Provincial Militia", "lat": 42.187378, "lng": -72.629561, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/e_chapin.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Born July 16, 1707, Elisha Chapin was a rugged provincial militia officer who lived and fought long before the town of Holyoke or the United States existed. During the early colonial struggles, he served on the most exposed edge of the British Empire, commanding Fort Massachusetts along the Hoosac River frontier from 1752 to 1754. His post was the westernmost fortification defending British colonial Massachusetts from French and Native war parties sweeping down from Canada, a turbulent region where scouting patrols, sudden ambushes, and constant vigilance were facts of daily life. On the humid afternoon of July 11, 1756, as the French and Indian War escalated across the frontier, Captain Chapin ventured outside the safety of the West Hoosac Blockhouse near Hemlock Brook to track down a herd of strayed cattle. Walking into a meticulously laid ambush, he and his two companions were killed by a party of Native warriors loyal to the French forces before the garrison could mount a rescue. His remains were later returned to his family's ancestral grounds in the valley. Today, he rests at Elmwood Cemetery, where his weathered brownstone headstone stands as one of the oldest military monuments in the region. Its chilling, historic inscription—\"In Memory of Capt. Elisha Chapin who was killed by the savages at Williamstown\"—endures as a stark testament to the bloodiest years of the colonial frontier. Geolocation of the exact grave site remains pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": ["KIA"], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "jno_eveleth_cw", "name": "John Eveleth", "era": "Civil War", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.186958, "lng": -72.629272, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/eveleth.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "John Eveleth — listed on his marker as Jno. Eveleth — served with the 15th Massachusetts Battery during the Civil War. The 15th Massachusetts Battery was a light artillery unit that served in the Eastern Theater, providing cannon support in engagements across Virginia and Maryland. Artillery units like the 15th were essential to Union battlefield tactics, delivering fire that could determine the outcome of an engagement. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "arthur_emil_ezold_wwii", "name": "Arthur Emil Ezold", "era": "World War II", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.186986, "lng": -72.629242, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/ezold.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Arthur Emil Ezold served as a Sergeant in the 1460th Service Command Unit of the United States Army during World War II. Service command units were the logistical backbone of the American war effort, managing the supply chains, depots, and transport networks that kept combat forces equipped and operational across multiple theaters. Ezold was born June 15, 1906 and died September 27, 1967. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "fred_k_burnham_cw", "name": "Frederick K. Burnham", "era": "Civil War", "branch": "Massachusetts Militia", "branchLabel": "Massachusetts Militia", "lat": 42.187283, "lng": -72.629058, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/f_burnham.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Born in New York around 1841, Frederick K. Burnham—chiseled on his marker simply as Fred'k Burnham—was a twenty-year-old local laborer living in Holyoke when the fracturing of the Union upended civilian life. When the call for volunteers rippled through New England, he left his daily routine to enlist on June 21, 1861. Burnham was a private assigned to Company I of the 10th Massachusetts Volunteer Infantry, a celebrated regiment primarily raised from the working-class towns and mills of western Massachusetts. Burnham was thrust directly into the crucible of the land war, marching through some of the Eastern Theater's most devastating and consequential campaigns. Through the noise and smoke of combat, his unit stood firm in the mud and trenches of the Peninsular Campaign, survived the slaughter of Antietam, and endured the grinding attrition of the Overland Campaign. After completing a grueling three-year enlistment, Frederick was honorably mustered out of the service on July 1, 1864. He returned to civilian life in Holyoke, carrying the memories of the great national struggle, passing away in the post-war era. He was laid to rest at Elmwood Cemetery. Today, an iron Grand Army of the Republic (G.A.R.) marker stands watch beside his final resting place. Geolocation of his individual grave plot remains pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "david_w_mulligan_unk", "name": "David W. Mulligan", "era": "Unknown Era", "branch": "", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187531, "lng": -72.628983, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/d_mulligan.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Born in 1918, David Mulligan belonged to a generation that would bear the heavy burden of preserving democracy. His life spanned from the localized industrial hum of the Paper City to the global theaters of modern warfare. As a young man in his prime, he came of age during a time when millions of American citizens were abruptly transformed from factory workers, farmers, and students into disciplined soldiers, sailors, and airmen. Mulligan was just 23 on December 7, 1941, when the surprise attack on Pearl Harbor thrust the United States directly into World War II. While his deployment, branch, and unit assignment are unknown at this writing, he lived through the high-stakes mobilization and weathered the dangerous geopolitics of the subsequent Korean War era, a 15-year period of unmatched international tension that reshaped the global landscape. David W. Mulligan passed away in 1965 at the young age of 47. Beside his grave, an American flag stands vigilant in the grass, placed there by the community to honor his status as a defender of the nation. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "oswald_f_friedrich", "name": "Oswald F. Friedrich", "era": "Unknown Era", "branch": "Unknown", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187283, "lng": -72.628983, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/friedrich.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Oswald F. Friedrich was born in 1862 and died in 1947, having lived through the Civil War, the Spanish-American War, both World Wars, and into the postwar era. A U.S. veteran marker stands at his grave at Elmwood Cemetery. Born in 1862, Friedrich was of enlistment age during the Spanish-American War of 1898 and may also have served during the era of the Philippine Insurrection that followed. His specific branch of service and unit have not yet been confirmed from available records. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "george_a_baush_wwii", "name": "George A. Baush", "era": "World War II", "branch": "Coast Guard", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187089, "lng": -72.62915, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/g_baush.jpg", "extraPhotos": [{"src": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/g_baush_portrait.jpg", "caption": "Portrait of George Baush"}], "narrative": "In the late 1930s and early 1940s, Holyoke hummed with civic activity, closely tied to local schools, neighborhood parishes, and the city's proud musical traditions. Born in Holyoke on January 2, 1928, George A. \"Snizz\" Baush grew up as the son of Carlton and Mary Barrett Baush. He walked the city's historic streets with an early eye for civic duty and a deep talent for music, playing the trumpet with local groups before graduating from Holyoke High School. When the second World War expanded globally, George stepped forward to enlist in the United States Coast Guard, as the Coast Guard transformed from a peacetime shore guardian into a critical front-line instrument of global warfare. Seaman Baush entered the service just as its wartime duties exploded, pushing crews into the U-boat-infested waters of the North Atlantic to shield supply convoys and anchoring strict port security measures to protect industrial heartlands at home. In every theater, Coast Guardsmen faced perilous tasks, often steering the vulnerable, flat-bottomed landing craft that delivered thousands of troops directly onto heavily fortified shores. After surviving the war and witnessing the dawn of peace, George returned home to build a vibrant civilian legacy deeply rooted in education and public service, earning a Bachelor's Degree from Fitchburg State College and a Master's Degree from Westfield State College. For many years, he molded the minds of local youth as a beloved teacher at Chestnut Street Junior High School in Springfield. True to his musical roots, \"Snizz\" remained a prominent figure in the local cultural landscape, playing his trumpet with the Melha Shriners and performing with various area bands as a proud member of the Musician's Local #171. Though he faced the early heartbreak of losing his wife, Patricia Chaput Baush, in 1971, George poured his heart into his family and community, becoming affectionately known to many outside his household simply as \"Grampy\". He lived a long, full life defined by his love for gardening, birdwatching, and visits to the ocean. George A. \"Snizz\" Baush died Thursday October 15, 2015 at the age of 87 at Holyoke Medical Center, surrounded by his loving family. He was laid to rest with full military honors in Elmwood Cemetery. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "g_burnham_cw", "name": "George Burnham", "era": "Civil War", "branch": "Massachusetts Militia", "branchLabel": "Massachusetts Militia", "lat": 42.187283, "lng": -72.629036, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/g_burnham.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Born in Hanover, New Hampshire, around 1833, George Burnham moved to the Pioneer Valley as a young man, embedding himself in the local trades before the fracturing of the Union upended civilian life. When the Civil War erupted, he answered the call to arms from Holyoke, his adopted home, enlisting on June 21, 1861, at the age of 28. He was assigned as a drummer to Company I of the 10th Massachusetts Volunteer Infantry, a regiment primarily raised from the working-class towns of Western Massachusetts. Burnham's musical skill quickly brought a promotion to Principal Musician for the regiment. On the battlefields of the Eastern Theater, Burnham carried a heavy tactical burden. Regimental musicians did not operate in the safety of rear camps; they marched directly into the smoke of combat, using their drum cadences and signals as the primary communication network to relay vital orders, maneuvers, and charges over the deafening roar of gunfire. Through a grueling three-year enlistment, he drummed his regiment through the bloody campaigns of the Peninsula, the horrors of the Wilderness, and the brutal infantry clashes at the \"Bloody Angle\" of Spotsylvania. He survived his term and was mustered out on July 1, 1864, right as his unit engaged in the initial assaults at Petersburg. Returning to Massachusetts, Burnham lived out his peaceful post-war years as a respected veteran of the great national struggle. He was laid to rest at Elmwood Cemetery, though a definitive geolocation of his grave site is pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "gooley_unknown", "name": "Gooley", "era": "Unknown Era", "branch": "Unknown", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187422, "lng": -72.629144, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/gooley.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "This veteran's stone at Elmwood Cemetery is too worn to read clearly. An American flag marks the grave, confirming veteran status. Name may be Gooley. Branch of service and era have not yet been confirmed. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "sa_hall_cw", "name": "S.A. Hall", "era": "Civil War", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187461, "lng": -72.628814, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/hall.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "S.A. Hall served in Company B of the 46th Massachusetts Volunteer Infantry during the Civil War. The 46th Massachusetts was organized in the fall of 1862 as a nine-month regiment, one of many raised to meet the Union Army's urgent need for manpower following early setbacks in the war. It served in North Carolina under General John Foster, participating in operations aimed at disrupting Confederate supply lines and controlling the sounds and ports of the Carolina coast. The regiment mustered out in September 1863, having completed its term of service. A GAR marker at the grave recognizes Hall's Union service. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "hupfer_unknown", "name": "Hupfer", "era": "Unknown Era", "branch": "Unknown", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187003, "lng": -72.629211, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/hupper1.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "A U.S. War Veteran marker stands at the Hupfer family stone at Elmwood Cemetery. The specific veteran's first name, branch of service, and era have not yet been confirmed from available records. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "john_hynd_unknown", "name": "John Hynd", "era": "Unknown Era", "branch": "Unknown", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187236, "lng": -72.629578, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/hynd.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "John Hynd was born August 18, 1893 and died February 20, 1971. An American flag marks his grave at Elmwood Cemetery, confirming his veteran status. Born in 1893, Hynd was 23 years old when the United States entered World War I in April 1917, placing him squarely within the generation that fought on the Western Front with the American Expeditionary Forces. He was also 48 years old at the time of Pearl Harbor, within the upper range of wartime service age. His branch of service and unit assignment have not yet been confirmed from available records. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "capt_joseph_day_rev", "name": "Day, Joseph", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187106, "lng": -72.629914, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/j_day.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "[FIRST DRAFT] Captain Joseph Day went to the war as a private, in Captain Joseph Morgan's company of Colonel John Moseley's regiment — out from October 21 to November 17, 1776, twenty-eight days to reinforce the Northern Army under Lieutenant Colonel Timothy Robinson of Granville, the roll dated at West Springfield. He was out again with the same company from September 21 to October 17, 1777: one month and three days on the expedition to the northern department. The rank on his stone came later; the rolls have him a private. His brownstone at Elmwood, carved with an urn and willow, records that he died March 19, 1813, aged 67, and carries also Mrs. Azubah Day, his consort, who died December 11, 1786, aged 37 — twenty-seven years before him. A newspaper account of the Day family wonders whether he might have been the father of Eunice Day. He cannot have been: Eunice was eleven years his senior. A 1929 survey placed him in the fifteenth row from the South Drive, marker 14. He was formerly carried in this archive twice — once from the survey of his stone and once from a list of Revolutionary graves. The two are one man, and the duplicate has been removed. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "james_humeston_rev", "name": "Humeston, James", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187344, "lng": -72.629303, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/j_humeston.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "James Humeston served during the Revolutionary War era and is honored at Elmwood Cemetery in Holyoke, where both an American flag and a Holyoke Lodge marker stand at his wife Martha's stone. The Connecticut River Valley contributed significantly to the Revolutionary War effort: Massachusetts militia units from this region served at Bunker Hill, in the defense of Boston, and in the northern campaigns that culminated in the American victory at Saratoga in 1777. James Humeston's specific regiment and dates of service have not yet been confirmed from available records. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "josiah_rogers_rev", "name": "Rogers, Josiah", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187431, "lng": -72.629539, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/josiahrogers_com.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Deacon Josiah Rogers was a young man of faith and fortitude whose life bridge-linked the closing campaigns of the War for Independence with the early, hardscrabble settlement of Western Massachusetts. Born around 1764, he came of age during the tumultuous height of the Revolutionary War. Today, his final resting place is honored at Elmwood Cemetery. As a teenager growing up in New England during the War for Independence, Josiah belonged to a generation that was thrust early into the burdens of adulthood. The historical presence of a veteran marker at his grave testifies to his active participation in the conflict, likely serving as a youthful recruit in the local militia or a provincial regiment during the final, grueling years of the Revolutionary War. In an era when teenagers routinely took up arms to guard supply routes, secure frontiers, or reinforce the Continental Army, young men like Josiah formed a critical, defensive line that secured American self-governance. Following the treaty of peace, Josiah embraced the grueling work of a pioneer, earning the respected title of \"Deacon\" within the local parish—a testament to his moral character and standing in the growing community. Josiah's story carries a poignant reminder of the fragile nature of early American life. The inscription detailing his wife Sarah's passing in September 1779 at Waterbury, Connecticut, connects his family history back to the historic Connecticut River Valley corridors. In a time of primitive medicine and unrelenting physical labor, families frequently suffered devastating, early losses as they pushed northward to clear new farmsteads. Deacon Josiah Rogers passed away in December 1792, cut down in his prime at the age of 28. Though his life was brief, his impact was etched permanently into the landscape. At Elmwood Cemetery, his weathered headstone stands amongst the early founders of the region. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "robert_alfred_krause_wwii", "name": "Robert Alfred Krause", "era": "World War II", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187417, "lng": -72.629311, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/krause.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Robert Alfred Krause served as a Sergeant in the United States Army during World War II and was awarded the Purple Heart for wounds received in combat. The Purple Heart, authorized by George Washington in 1782, is awarded to members of the armed forces wounded or killed in action against an enemy of the United States. Receiving it as a Sergeant indicates Krause served in a leadership role at the squad or section level, typically responsible for a small group of men under fire. Krause was born February 11, 1918 and died November 1, 1991. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": ["Purple Heart"], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "lt_martin_chapin_rev", "name": "Chapin, Martin", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Massachusetts Militia", "branchLabel": "Massachusetts Militia", "lat": 42.187131, "lng": -72.629922, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/m_chapin.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Born in West Springfield around 1744, Martin Chapin spent his youth navigating the quiet agrarian frontier along the Connecticut River. He established a household in the valley after marrying Bathsheba Cooper in 1768, tilling his fields until the outbreak of the American Revolution. As the colonies fractured, Martin Chapin stepped forward to serve as a non-commissioned officer in Captain Joseph Morgan's company of Colonel John Moseley's 3rd Hampshire County Regiment. In July 1776, Sergeant Chapin marched his squad north into the grueling wilderness of the Northern Theater, serving as a vital defensive shield at outposts like Fort Ticonderoga—to blunt the threat of British advances down the Hudson. In September 1777, Chapin slung his pack a second time with the same company, marching straight into the monumental Saratoga Campaign. Facing the immense tension of the front lines during the decisive weeks of maneuvering, his unit helped encircle and secure the historic surrender of British General Burgoyne's army. He was honorably discharged on the day of the British capitulation, October 17, 1777. Returning to civilian life, Martin managed his local farm through the dawn of the new republic, passing away in April 1793 in his 50th year. Today, his life remains anchored at Elmwood Cemetery, where his beautifully carved brownstone features a classic New England \"winged soul's head\" nestled between two rosettes, marking it as one of the most striking early monuments in the burial ground. A 1929 survey placed his stone in the eighteenth row. Geolocation of the exact grave site is pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "jno_montgomery_cw", "name": "John Montgomery", "era": "Civil War", "branch": "Co. C, 25th New Jersey Volunteer Infantry", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.186914, "lng": -72.628739, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/montgomery.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "John Montgomery—listed on his weathered headstone under the historical abbreviation \"Jno. Montgomery\"—served as a Private in Company C of the 25th New Jersey Volunteer Infantry, organized at Camp Cadwallader in Beverly, New Jersey, during the anxious autumn of September 1862, to fulfill a brief nine-month enlistment. Despite the short duration of their term, Montgomery and his comrades under Colonel Andrew Derrom were thrown directly into the teeth of the war. Initially assigned to the grim, muddy fortifications defending Washington, D.C., Company C soon marched south to reinforce the battle-weary lines of the Virginia theater. Montgomery's regiment took a conspicuous and bloody part in the catastrophic Battle of Fredericksburg on December 13, 1862. Positioned as part of the Ninth Corps of the Army of the Potomac, the 25th New Jersey braved heavy artillery fire and desperate frontal assaults, sustaining substantial casualties with 9 killed, 58 wounded, and 18 missing during the brutal winter operations. Montgomery then survived the infamous, bone-chilling cold of Major General Ambrose Burnside's disastrous \"Mud March\" in January 1863 before his unit was transferred to southeastern Virginia. There, they faced the grinding hardships of trench warfare, digging fortifications, and engaging in sharp enemy skirmishes during the grueling Siege of Suffolk from April to May 1863. After executing their duties honorably, Montgomery and Company C traveled back to New Jersey, where they officially mustered out of federal service on June 20, 1863. Following the trauma of the battlefields, Montgomery joined a grand post-war migration of Civil War veterans who left their home states to seek rebirth in New England's thriving industrial centers. John Montgomery settled in the Pioneer Valley, drawn to Holyoke—the burgeoning \"Paper City\"—whose massive, water-powered mill economy offered a stable livelihood to soldiers looking to rebuild their civilian lives. Today, he rests permanently among the trees of Elmwood Cemetery, a testament to a generation that fought to preserve the Union and stayed to build the American industrial frontier. Geolocation is pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "unknown_soldier_1", "name": "Unknown Soldier 1", "era": "Unknown Era", "branch": "Unknown", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187164, "lng": -72.629944, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/fmo.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "This veteran's stone at Elmwood Cemetery is too worn to read. An American flag marks the grave, confirming veteran status. Name, branch, and era have not yet been confirmed. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "capt_joseph_morgan_colonial", "name": "Morgan, Joseph", "era": ["French & Indian War", "Revolutionary War"], "branch": "Captain, 5th Co., Col. John Moseley's Regiment", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187164, "lng": -72.629706, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/morgan.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Captain Joseph Morgan was a warrior of two distinct eras whose legacy reflects a life spent on the bloody, shifting frontiers of early America. Born on February 19, 1736, in the West Parish of Springfield (later West Springfield), he grew up to become one of Ireland Parish's most influential political and military leaders. He stands at the root of a massive American empire, serving as the great-great-grandfather of Wall Street financier J.P. Morgan. Today, his final resting place is Elmwood Cemetery, where his weathered gravestone—beautifully carved by the historic regional artisan John Ely—carries a harrowing inscription detailing the first conflict of his youth: he was \"captured and included in the capitulation of Fort Wm. Henry Aug. 1757.\" At just 21 years old, Joseph was serving as a colonial provincial soldier for the British Empire when he was stationed at Fort William Henry on the southern tip of Lake George, New York. In August 1757, a French army of 8,000 regulars and nearly 2,000 Native warriors under General Montcalm surrounded the isolated fort. Following a brutal six-day bombardment, the British garrison surrendered under a treaty promising safe passage. Instead, Montcalm's Indigenous allies ambushed the retreating column. Joseph was swept directly into the infamous Fort William Henry Massacre—a historical tragedy later immortalized in James Fenimore Cooper's novel The Last of the Mohicans. Joseph survived the brutal ambush and captivity, returned home to the valley, and later married Experience Smith on September 19, 1765. When the American Revolution erupted, Joseph turned his weapon against the very empire he had defended as a young man. Local accounts note that he personally rallied Ireland Parish's very first recruits—folding his neighbors, his younger brother Lucas Morgan, and his son Erastus into the Third Hampshire County Regiment. On April 30, 1776, he was officially commissioned as Captain of the 5th Company under Colonel John Moseley. From October 21 to November 17, 1776, he commanded a detachment marching to reinforce the Northern Army at Fort Ticonderoga. He marched out again from September 21 to October 17, 1777, commanding his company during the critical Saratoga Campaign alongside his twin brother, Jesse Morgan. Later in life, Captain Morgan's battlefield leadership was called upon once more to quell domestic unrest during Shays' Rebellion (1786–1787). He answered the call to protect the government courthouse at Northampton under Lieutenant Colonel David Moseley, defending the young republic he had fought to establish. Captain Joseph Morgan passed away on December 18, 1813, in his 78th year, followed by his widow, Experience, in 1821. A certified military survey sealed at Boston on November 14, 1929, officially honors his dual legacy, listing him among the prominent Revolutionary soldiers buried at Elmwood Cemetery. He rests at Marker 12 in the 14th row from the South Drive, side-by-side with his brother Jesse. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "richard_j_powers_navy", "name": "Richard J. Powers", "era": "World War II", "branch": "Navy", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187325, "lng": -72.628822, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/powers.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Richard J. Powers belonged to the legendary generation of sailors in the United States Navy during the height of global conflict. Born on February 20, 1914, Richard was already a mature 27-year-old man when the United States plunged into World War II following the attack on Pearl Harbor. Today, his service to the nation is quietly commemorated at Elmwood Cemetery. Powers earned the rank of Fireman First Class (F1c), a vital and intensely grueling role within the Navy's engineering force. Far below the open deck, safely removed from the horizon but trapped in the belly of the vessel, Navy firemen were responsible for the mechanical heartbeat of the ship. They did not man the deck guns or peer through binoculars; instead, they operated and maintained the monstrous, high-pressure steam boilers, turbines, and complex propulsion systems. In combat conditions, their job was one of pure grit. As ships veered into aggressive maneuvers to dodge enemy torpedoes or kamikaze dives, the temperature in the firerooms could soar past 120 degrees Fahrenheit. If a boiler failed or a line ruptured, the ship lost headway, transforming a massive warship into a sitting duck. It was the unyielding vigilance of firemen like Powers that kept American vessels underway, maneuvering, and fighting through the worst storms of the Pacific and Atlantic theaters. Surviving the harrowing risks of the war, Powers returned home to witness decades of peace in a nation he helped secure. He passed away on January 13, 1984, at the age of 69. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "raymond_f_humeston_wwi", "name": "Raymond F. Humeston", "era": "World War I", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187397, "lng": -72.629342, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/r_humeston.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Raymond F. Humeston served as a Private in the United States Army during World War I. He entered service on August 31, 1918, and was stationed at Camp Jackson, South Carolina — one of the great wartime training camps where hundreds of thousands of American soldiers were prepared for the front. Born in 1895, Humeston was among the young men of Holyoke who answered the call, though the armistice of November 1918 came before many of the late-summer draftees could ship overseas. He died in 1982, having lived nearly a century. He may be related to James Humeston, a Revolutionary War veteran also buried at Elmwood, though the connection is not confirmed. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "otto_ernest_riedel_wwii", "name": "Otto Ernest Riedel", "era": "World War II", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187381, "lng": -72.629272, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/riedel.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Otto Ernest Riedel served as a Private in the United States Army during World War II. Born in 1911 and 30 years old at the time of Pearl Harbor, Riedel served within the broad mobilization that expanded the Army from fewer than 200,000 men in 1939 to more than eight million by 1945. It was ordinary citizens stepping into the uniform of an Army Private who bore the staggering logistical and combat weight of this rapid mobilization. Privates like Riedel left behind their civilian careers to form the essential backbone of the military, driving across the desert sands of North Africa, enduring the freezing hedge-rows of Western Europe, and island-hopping across the brutal tropical terrains of the Pacific theater. Following the Allied victory, Riedel returned to civilian life, witnessing decades of subsequent peace and the steady industrial growth along the Connecticut River. He passed away in July 1985 at the age of 74. Riedel is buried within the historic Hupfer family plot at Elmwood Cemetery. His government marker is a permanent tribute to a citizen-soldier who answered the call when the global line between tyranny and freedom hung in the balance. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "lawrence_w_robinson_wwii", "name": "Lawrence W. Robinson", "era": "World War II", "branch": "Army Air Forces", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187458, "lng": -72.629836, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/robinson.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Lawrence W. Robinson was a member of the elite generation that took to the skies and revolutionized modern warfare during global conflict. Born on November 27, 1923, he was just 18 years old when the world was thrown into chaos following the United States' entry into World War II. Robinson earned the rank of Private First Class (PFC) while serving in the United States Army Air Forces (USAAF), the military's aerial warfare service and the predecessor to the modern, independent United States Air Force. While pilots, navigators, and bombardiers captured the public's imagination from the cockpits, the entire system relied heavily on the unyielding work ethic of ground crews and support personnel. As a PFC, Robinson belonged to the vital network of personnel who kept the massive air bases functioning under tight, high-stakes military schedules. Ground personnel handled everything from weapons loading, aircraft maintenance, and runway logistics to communications and critical supply operations. Whether stationed at a bustling training airfield stateside or deployed to a forward operating base in the European or Pacific theaters, it was the daily dedication of servicemen like Robinson that ensured American fighters and bombers could launch, strike, and return safely. Robinson survived the dangerous landscape of the war years, returning to civilian life to witness decades of post-war prosperity and the transformation of the 20th century, passing away February 22, 2002, at the age of 78. His final resting place stands as a permanent reminder of the young men from Holyoke who stepped forward to secure the skies. Carved into his veteran history, his rank and branch tell the enduring story of a citizen-soldier who helped build the very foundation of American air power when the freedom of the world depended on it. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "elsie_a_schenker_wwi", "name": "Elsie A. Schenker", "era": "World War I", "branch": "Navy", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187319, "lng": -72.628722, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/schenker.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Elsie A. Schenker served as a Yeoman Second Class — Yeoman (F) — in the United States Navy during World War I, making her among the first women in American history to serve in the military in a non-nursing capacity. The program began in March 1917, when Secretary of the Navy Josephus Daniels, finding no law requiring that a yeoman be a man, began enlisting women into the Naval Reserve. More than eleven thousand women ultimately served, popularly known as Yeomanettes — a nickname many of them disliked, preferring simply to be called Sailors. They worked as stenographers, bookkeepers, telephone operators, translators, fingerprint experts, and camouflage designers; a few served in France. They received equal pay with men of the same rank and served under the same military regulations. Schenker was born November 21, 1890 and died June 17, 1976. She is the only female veteran documented at Elmwood Cemetery. All Yeoman (F) enlistments formally ended in October 1920, and women would not serve in the uniformed Navy again until 1942. Elsie Schenker’s gravestone at Elmwood Cemetery is a record of a moment when the country asked something new of its women, and they answered. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": ["Female Veteran"], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "alfred_b_sefton_wwii", "name": "Alfred B. Sefton", "era": "World War II", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187594, "lng": -72.629417, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/sefton.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "In the decades following the First World War, the residential blocks branching away from Holyoke's dense city center provided a stable, working-class foundation for local families. Born on January 26, 1919, Alfred B. Sefton grew up navigating the industrial and cultural rhythms of the Paper City. He was twenty-two when the United States entered its second global conflict in December 1941. Leaving his civilian routine behind, Alfred stepped forward to answer the national call to arms, entering the United States Army to support the massive Allied mobilization. He earned the rank of Staff Sergeant, a non-commissioned officer designation carrying significant tactical responsibilities in the field. Within land-based infantry or weapons units, a Staff Sergeant served as a critical line leader, tasked with directing squad movements, managing weapons teams, and ensuring tight unit cohesion under operational stress. These junior leaders operated in close support and frontline roles, executing vital orders and making localized tactical adjustments when officers were absent. Alfred survived the hardships of the second world war and safely returned home to the Pioneer Valley, settling into post-war civilian life as the local landscape stabilized. Alfred married Ruth E. Deacon, a lifelong Holyoke resident who would later dedicate many years of service as an administrative assistant at the local Holyoke Soldiers' Home. The couple established their home at 29 Elmwood Avenue in Holyoke, raising their children—Robin, Jeffrey, and Mark—within the same close-knit community fabric that had shaped their own youth. Tragically, they also bore the heartbreak of losing a fourth son, Russell, in 1969. Alfred lived a long life surrounded by his family and neighborhood roots, until his passing at the age of 80 on September 19, 1999. Today, he rests in Holyoke's historically protected Elmwood Cemetery, his legacy permanently bound to the \"great generation\" of citizen-soldiers who returned from war to sustain the community long after the guns fell silent. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "william_f_tauscher_wwi", "name": "William F. Tauscher", "era": "World War I", "branch": "Navy", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.186961, "lng": -72.629614, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/tauscher.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "William F. Tauscher rests in the quiet, historic grounds of Elmwood Cemetery in Holyoke, Massachusetts, his final watch safeguarded by a legacy of naval service. Born on September 4, 1894, Tauscher was a young man of 22 when the United States plunged into World War I in the spring of 1917. Answering the call of the sea, he enlisted in the United States Naval Reserve Force (USNRF), joining a massive mobilization that drew thousands of patriotic young men from across the Commonwealth to man the ships of a rapidly expanding fleet. While many answered the call to man the heavy guns or stoke the boilers, Tauscher took on a vital, demanding role essential to the survival and morale of the fleet: serving as a Cook. In the cramped, rolling galleys of the wartime Navy, keeping a crew fed and fit for duty was a monumental task. The USNRF expanded at a breakneck pace to counter the deadly German U-boat threat in the Atlantic, and cooks like Tauscher worked tirelessly around the clock, battling rough seas, tight rations, and the constant threat of naval warfare to sustain the sailors who kept the sea lanes open. Following his honorable service in the Great War, Tauscher returned to civilian life, witnessing the decades of profound change that shaped the 20th century before his passing on January 10, 1963. Today, his resting place among the veterans of Elmwood Cemetery stands as a permanent tribute to his dedication. His headstone serves as a proud reminder that every role in the armed forces—whether on the firing line or in the galley—was woven into the fabric of the Allied victory, ensuring his service to the nation is permanently preserved in the soil of Holyoke. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "unknown_soldier_2", "name": "Unknown Soldier 2", "era": "Civil War", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.18695, "lng": -72.629172, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/unknown_vet_2.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "This Civil War veteran's stone at Elmwood Cemetery is too worn to read clearly. The standard Union Army shield marker shape and partial text suggest Massachusetts service. Name and unit have not yet been confirmed. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "unknown_soldier_3", "name": "Unknown Soldier 3", "era": "Unknown Era", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187428, "lng": -72.629144, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/vet_unknown3.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "This veteran's identity has not yet been confirmed from the stone or available records. The headstone has been photographed and geolocated to preserve the record of an unidentified soldier's presence in this burial ground. If you can identify this veteran, please contact the Veterans Graves Officer so the record can be completed. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "ernest_j_hupfer", "name": "Ernest J. Hupfer", "era": "Unknown Era", "branch": "Unknown", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187394, "lng": -72.629319, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/vetunknown4.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Ernest J. Hupfer was born in 1883 and died in 1954. An American flag marks the Hupfer family stone at Elmwood Cemetery, indicating veteran status. Born in 1883, Hupfer was 34 years old when the United States entered World War I in 1917, within the eligible range for military service under the Selective Service Act of that year, which applied to men ages 21 to 31 at first registration, later expanded to 18 to 45. He may be the veteran recognized by the flag at this plot, though a second family member, also a veteran, is listed nearby. His branch of service and unit assignment have not yet been confirmed from available records. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "frank_h_wade", "name": "Frank H. Wade", "era": "Unknown Era", "branch": "Unknown", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187133, "lng": -72.629953, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/wade_batchelor.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Frank H. Wade lies in the quiet landscape of Elmwood Cemetery, his final resting place marked by the silent, steady flutter of an American flag. Born in 1872 and passing in 1952, Wade lived through a transformative era of American history, a lifetime bounded by two major global conflicts. The precise details of his branch of service and unit assignment are TBD, but the American flag planted firmly beside his headstone ensures his devotion to the nation is never forgotten. Wade would have been just 25 years old when the Spanish-American War erupted in 1898, placing him at the perfect age for military service during a time when patriotic fervor swept across the Commonwealth, contributing thousands of eager volunteers to the war effort. Many were deployed to the humid, tropical fronts of Cuba, Puerto Rico, and the Philippines. Whether Wade braved the elements in the Caribbean or faced the challenges of the Pacific theater, his youth was defined by a nation stepping onto the world stage. By the time the United States entered World War I in 1917, Wade was a mature 44 years old. The exact records of his service are yet TBD. Wade lived out a long life, witnessing the turn of a new century before finding his peace back in the Pioneer Valley. He was laid to rest beside his wife Mary Batchelor, who passed eight years earlier. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "paul_f_wagner", "name": "Paul F. Wagner", "era": "World War I", "branch": "Unknown", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187386, "lng": -72.629539, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/wagner.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "Paul F. Wagner rests within the peaceful, shaded terrain of Elmwood Cemetery, his final resting place permanently honored by a weathered veteran marker. Born in 1889 and passing away in 1945, Wagner's life spanned a tumultuous half-century defined by the two most devastating conflicts in human history. His graveside marker stands as a proud, enduring testament to a generation that answered the call to duty when the world descended into total war. Wagner was 27 years old when the United States officially entered World War I, placing him squarely within the historic first American citizens required to register under the Selective Service Act. As a young man in the prime of his life, he joined the ranks of over 189,000 Massachusetts men and women who stepped forward to serve. Though the passage of time has obscured his specific branch of service and unit assignment, he was part of a massive mobilization that transformed everyday citizens into the Doughboys who crossed the Atlantic to fight on the muddy, shell-shocked battlefields of Europe. Wagner's story came to a close in 1945, the monumental final year of World War II. While history has not yet confirmed whether his death was tied directly to wartime service or the physical tolls of his era's combat, the timing adds a poignant gravity to his legacy. He passed just as a new generation of soldiers succeeded in bringing about the global peace he had once fought to secure. Today, Paul rests beside his wife Sarah Palmer. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "lo_white_cw", "name": "L.O. White", "era": "Civil War", "branch": "Army", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187256, "lng": -72.629111, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/white_jbg.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "L.O. White rests beneath a standard Union shield marker at Elmwood Cemetery, his stone standing shoulder-to-shoulder with fellow Civil War veterans. When the fires of the rebellion threatened to tear the nation apart, White answered the call to arms, enlisting in Company B of the 46th Massachusetts Volunteer Infantry. Organized in the autumn of 1862, this nine-month regiment was forged from the communities of Western Massachusetts, binding White to a brotherhood of local patriots. Leaving the rolling hills of the Pioneer Valley behind, White and the men of the 46th Massachusetts were deployed into the humid, perilous landscape of North Carolina. Company B faced the grueling realities of Civil War campaigning, from strategic maneuvers along the Neuse River to the sharp, chaotic clashes at Kinston, Whitehall, and Goldsboro. Their mission was vital: disrupting Confederate supply lines and holding the line for the Union. Though the regiment's term was short compared to multi-year units, the tropical fevers, brutal swamp marches, and flash of enemy musketry exacted a heavy toll on these New England boys. When their nine months expired in the summer of 1863, White returned home to a grateful Commonwealth, carrying the invisible weight of the war with him. Today, his final watch is kept in the quiet, shaded expanse of Elmwood Cemetery. His weathered marble headstone, etched with the iconic Union shield, remains a silent sentinel of freedom. It serves as a permanent visual anchor to the era when ordinary local citizens left their trades to preserve the Union, ensuring that the legacy of Company B is never forgotten by the city he helped safeguard. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "unknown_soldier_4", "name": "Unknown Soldier 4", "era": "Unknown Era", "branch": "Unknown", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187156, "lng": -72.629953, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/rmday.jpg", "extraPhotos": [], "narrative": "This veteran's identity has not yet been confirmed from the stone or available records. The headstone has been photographed and geolocated to preserve the record, and this entry is maintained as a record of an unidentified soldier's presence in this burial ground. If you can identify this veteran, please contact the Veterans Graves Officer so the record can be completed. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "elmwood-migrate"}, {"id": "harper_rev_ball_charles", "name": "Ball, Charles", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Massachusetts Militia", "branchLabel": "Massachusetts Militia", "lat": 42.187317, "lng": -72.6293, "photo": "", "narrative": "Charles Ball walked a landscape thick with family history long before he ever answered the call to arms. A sergeant in the Revolutionary War, Ball belonged to a lineage deeply rooted in Holyoke's soil. His forebears, Francis and Captain Eli Ball, had claimed lots in this frontier territory around 1707, carving out a homestead near what modern maps call Northampton and Cherry Streets. For generations, locals knew this bustling crossroads simply as Ball's Corner. In 1751, a member of the family planted a solitary elm tree there—a towering sentinel that grew so massive it remained a fixture on regional maps long after the planter had passed. Life in those early colonial years was dictated by vigilance. When the threat of raids shadowed the valley, six foundational families—the Balls, Chapins, Days, Elys, Millers, and Morgans—would tightly fort together under the cover of night, finding safety in numbers and shared courage. Today, that wartime camaraderie has transformed into an eternal neighborhood; nearly every one of those historic surnames rests just a few rows away from Charles. Charles eventually married Merab Miller, weaving his story even tighter into the fabric of the community before his death on July 3, 1838. Sarah Rusk's record of Revolutionary patriots proudly preserves Charles's rank as a sergeant; the specific company and regiment he marched with have slipped into the shadows of time. According to a 1929 survey of the burial ground, his white marble stone is nestled in the eighteenth row from the South Drive, cataloged as marker 16. Sergeant Ball's grave has not yet been geolocated; the current digital pin remains provisional. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "sourceNote": "", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_chapin_asahel", "name": "Chapin, Asahel", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Massachusetts Militia", "branchLabel": "Massachusetts Militia", "lat": 42.187311, "lng": -72.629258, "photo": "", "narrative": "Born in Wilbraham on February 2, 1748, Asahel Chapin was the son of Stephen and Zebia Ely Chapin. As a young man, Asahel moved to the fertile valley of the old Third Parish, establishing a hardworking life as a farmer, long before the river was dammed to power mills. He was twenty-seven years old when the Colony's peace was shattered by shots fired at Lexington. Dropping his farm tools, Asahel moved with speed, marching on April 19, 1775, as a private in Captain Enoch Chapin's company of Minutemen. He stood proudly among the very first men of the parish to rush eastward to the defense of Boston. Chapin's devotion to the Patriot cause called him back to the ranks in September 1777, to join the 3rd Hampshire County Regiment to march north into the wilderness on an urgent expedition to reinforce Continental forces during the historic Saratoga Campaign, enduring a month of severe wilderness hardship before being honorably discharged on October 17, 1777—the day British General Burgoyne surrendered his entire army. Returning to his fields, Asahel married Sarah Frink to build a family. His deep roots in the community bloomed across generations; decades later in 1849, his son became the first pastor of Holyoke's newly formed Second Baptist Church. Asahel passed away on January 28, 1828, at the age of 80. He was laid to rest at Elmwood Cemetery, where a 1929 historical survey recorded his marble stone in the twelfth row. Geolocation of the exact grave site remains pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "sourceNote": "", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_day_jedediah", "name": "Day, Jedediah", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Massachusetts Militia", "branchLabel": "Massachusetts Militia", "lat": 42.187294, "lng": -72.629222, "photo": "", "narrative": "Born in West Springfield on March 7, 1754, Jedediah Day was another of the devoted sons of Joel and Eunice Day. He answered the call to arms at the very dawn of the American Revolution, enlisting on April 28, 1775—just days after the historic shots at Lexington and Concord shattered the colonial peace. Jedediah marched east to serve a vital three-month tour during the Siege of Boston. As the war grew more grueling, Jedediah repeatedly stepped back into the ranks, enduring seventy-four brutal days through the worst of the winter on a 1777 expedition to Fort Ticonderoga and volunteering for subsequent campaigns through the spring thaw of 1780. He survived the crucible of the war, though the victory was heavy with the grief of losing his brothers Edward and Robert. In the decades that followed, Jedediah built a prominent civilian life along the bustling banks of the Connecticut River. His homestead stood at a critical transit point where cargo bound upriver for Ireland Parish was landed; when the river waters ran too shallow for large vessels, ships unloaded at his doorstep, and he would summon local farmers with their oxen to cart the vital goods onward to Northampton. Deeply invested in his community, Jedediah was also one of the foundational pillars who gathered on Homestead Avenue in 1803 to establish the First Baptist Church. He lived to see the young nation flourish, passing away at the age of 85 on December 26, 1839. Jedediah Day was laid to rest at Elmwood Cemetery, where a 1929 survey placed his marble stone in the eleventh row. Geolocation of the exact grave site remains pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "sourceNote": "", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_day_joel", "name": "Day, Joel", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Massachusetts Militia", "branchLabel": "Massachusetts Militia", "lat": 42.187268, "lng": -72.629198, "photo": "/Veteran-Archives/elmwood/day_joel_sr.jpg", "narrative": "Born in West Springfield on April 6, 1730, Joel Day was the son of John Day and Abigail Bagg. Long before the heavy machinery of the industrial Paper City altered the landscape, he spent his decades tilling the soil of the old Third Parish—the quiet, rural farming frontier that would eventually become Holyoke. He was a mature forty-five-year-old farmer when the colonies fractured away from British rule. He moved with remarkable speed when the conflict began, marching out on April 20, 1775, as a Minuteman answering the Lexington alarm. Over the next five years, Joel repeatedly left his fields behind to answer sudden seasonal call-ups, marching north to reinforce the wilderness garrison at Fort Ticonderoga and spending long winter months guarding the vital military stores downriver at Springfield. Behind his long service lay a steep family sacrifice. He had married Eunice Day of Northfield in 1751, and five of their sons marched off to fight. Two of those boys never came home. Joel survived the war and died on February 14, 1803, in his seventy-third year. Today, his final resting place is preserved at Elmwood Cemetery, though a definitive physical geolocation of the grave site is still pending. His brownstone headstone carries a tragic postscript: carved directly beneath his own name are the names of his fallen sons, Edward and Robert—the only physical monument either boy ever received. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "sourceNote": "", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_day_joel_jr", "name": "Day, Joel, Jr.", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Massachusetts Militia", "branchLabel": "Massachusetts Militia", "lat": 42.187237, "lng": -72.62919, "photo": "", "narrative": "Born in West Springfield on August 15, 1751, Joel Day, Jr. was the eldest son of Joel and Eunice Day. He grew up working his family's farm in the Third Parish during an era when the local colonial community was completely isolated from major cities, bound tightly together by agriculture and the growing friction with British rule. In May 1777, at twenty-five years old, Joel stepped forward to enter the ranks of the Massachusetts Militia for the first time, slinging a musket and marching north on foot in an urgent military expedition to Fort Ticonderoga. By September that same year, Joel had gone out to face the British invasion a second time, joining the historic campaign at Saratoga. He stood his ground through the frantic maneuvering that forced General Burgoyne's surrender, witnessing the monumental victory that effectively turned the tide of the entire war. When the fighting up north ended, Joel walked the long paths back home to his familiar civilian routine in the valley. His younger brothers, Edward and Robert, were not so fortunate, losing their lives to fatal camp illnesses during that same violent summer campaign. Joel lived to see the independent republic stabilize and the rural parish lands begin their early expansion. Joel Day Jr. passed away on March 13, 1830, in his seventy-ninth year. He was laid to rest at Elmwood Cemetery, where a 1929 historical survey recorded his weathered marble stone in the twentieth row from the South Drive. Geolocation of the grave site remains pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "sourceNote": "", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_ely_benjamin", "name": "Ely, Benjamin", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Massachusetts Militia", "branchLabel": "Massachusetts Militia", "lat": 42.187206, "lng": -72.629198, "photo": "", "narrative": "Born around 1730, Benjamin Ely established himself as a man of immense consequence in the Pioneer Valley long before he ever wore a military uniform. A prominent leader in early civic affairs, he shaped the political landscape of the region, serving in the state legislature and sitting on the historic 1773 committee that sought to set the local territory apart from West Springfield—the opening argument of the long legislative debate that eventually birthed Holyoke. When the American Revolution broke out, he brought his sharp leadership to the cause, accepting a commission as a Major in Colonel John Moseley's 3rd Hampshire Regiment on February 8, 1776. In the autumn of that year, he marched his detachment north into the wilderness, responding to an urgent order from General Schuyler to reinforce the critical northern outpost at Fort Ticonderoga before returning home a month later. In the peaceful years of independence that followed, Ely's homestead in the scenic Ingleside section served as a bustling hub for the growing community. There, he managed his farm and kept a lively, well-known tavern. Demonstrating a rare spirit of quiet compassion, it was on this very land that Ely permitted the last remaining Native American in the area to live out his final years in peace. Official rolls list his wartime rank as Major, while his gravestone records him as a Colonel—honors earned over a lifetime of service in the early state militia. He died on December 23, 1802, at age 72. He rests at Elmwood Cemetery, where a 1929 survey recorded his brownstone marker in the twelfth row from the South Drive; geolocation of the exact site is pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "sourceNote": "", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_ely_enoch", "name": "Ely, Enoch", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Massachusetts Militia", "branchLabel": "Massachusetts Militia", "lat": 42.18718, "lng": -72.629222, "photo": "", "narrative": "Born in West Springfield around 1754, Enoch Ely was raised on a quiet farming tract along the banks of Tannery Brook. He was a young man of twenty-one when the colonial peace was shattered by the shots fired at Lexington and Concord. Moving with remarkable speed, Enoch answered the call to arms on April 28, 1775—just nine days after the initial alarm. He joined Captain Enoch Chapin's company in Colonel Timothy Danielson's regiment, marching east into a rapidly mobilizing province to serve through the historic summer when the raw American forces laid the siege of Boston, trapping the British army inside the city. Two years later, he took up his musket a second time, serving from September 21 to October 17, 1777, on the urgent expedition to the northern department to help choke off British movements in New York. With independence won, Enoch returned to the valley to build a lasting civilian legacy on the very soil he had defended. He reclaimed his land along Tannery Brook and became the builder and first owner of the historic homestead that later generations would affectionately call the Old Brown House. Ely lived to see the fragile young republic turn into an industrial powerhouse, passing away in 1842 at the age of 88. His marble headstone stands in the fourteenth row at Elmwood Cemetery. Geolocation of the exact grave site is pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "sourceNote": "", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_ely_joseph", "name": "Ely, Joseph", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Massachusetts Militia", "branchLabel": "Massachusetts Militia", "lat": 42.187163, "lng": -72.629258, "photo": "", "narrative": "Born in West Springfield on October 19, 1756, Joseph Ely spent his formative youth working the quiet, sprawling pastures of the Connecticut River Valley. He was twenty years old in the summer of 1777 when an urgent call to arms rippled through the county as British General Burgoyne attempted to sever New England from the rest of the colonies. Enlisting on July 9, 1777, the young man slung his musket and marched north into the wilderness to reinforce the Continental Northern Army, serving a brief but intense twenty-one days before his discharge. Though his military campaign was short, his long post-war life in the valley was monumental. He married Martha Chapin Smith and became a foundational pillar of the early community, helping to build and establish the First Baptist Church. When Joseph died on June 19, 1850, at the extraordinary age of ninety-three years and eight months, nearly three-quarters of a century had passed since his time in the ranks. In his final decades, he witnessed the extraordinary transformation of his hometown; the quiet, rural farmlands of his youth vanished, replaced by the roar of progress as the historic canals were cut and the massive industrial paper mills rose over the riverbanks. His weathered marble headstone remains preserved in the eleventh row at Elmwood Cemetery. Geolocation of the exact grave site is pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "sourceNote": "", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_ely_jube", "name": "Ely, Jube", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "", "branchLabel": "Revolutionary War", "lat": 42.187157, "lng": -72.6293, "photo": "", "narrative": "[FIRST DRAFT] Jube Ely enlisted July 28, 1779 in Captain Preserved Leonard's company of Colonel Elisha Porter's regiment and served one month and ten days at New London, Connecticut. The following year he was on the list of men raised to reinforce the Continental Army for a term of six months: he marched August 4, 1780 and was discharged December 15 — four months and seventeen days. He had a house on Northampton Street, shown on the map of 1827. Rusk's record and the 1929 survey of the burial ground both give the year of his death as 1843; a newspaper account of the Ely family says 1841. The two have not been reconciled and both are set down here. A 1929 survey placed his marble stone in the seventh row from the South Drive, marker 1 — the first of the twenty-one. This man's grave has not been surveyed. The pin is provisional — it marks the burial ground, not the grave.", "sourceNote": "Service record, from Wyatt E. Harper, The Story of Holyoke (published for the centennial of the City of Holyoke, 1973) — full researched narrative pending. Harper names him among the Revolutionary graves in this burial ground; the grave itself has not yet been located on the ground.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_fairfield_levi", "name": "Fairfield, Levi", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "", "branchLabel": "Revolutionary War", "lat": 42.187163, "lng": -72.629342, "photo": "", "narrative": "[FIRST DRAFT] Levi Fairfield fought in the Revolutionary War and died in 1817. A local account says plainly that little is known of him, and that is still true: his company, his regiment and his dates have not been recovered from the record. What is known is where he lies. A 1929 survey placed his marble stone in the twenty-second row from the South Drive, marker 19. The search for the rest of him continues. This man's grave has not been surveyed. The pin is provisional — it marks the burial ground, not the grave.", "sourceNote": "Service record, from Wyatt E. Harper, The Story of Holyoke (published for the centennial of the City of Holyoke, 1973) — full researched narrative pending. Harper names him among the Revolutionary graves in this burial ground; the grave itself has not yet been located on the ground.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_hitchcock_peresh", "name": "Hitchcock, Peresh", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "", "branchLabel": "Revolutionary War", "lat": 42.18718, "lng": -72.629378, "photo": "", "narrative": "[FIRST DRAFT] Peresh Hitchcock served in the Revolutionary War and died in 1831; his company, his regiment and his dates have not yet been recovered from the record. What is known is the life he built afterward. In 1803 he was one of the men who met at Caleb Humeston's house to help establish the Baptist Church; in 1805 he moderated a school meeting, and he was among those who established the Baptist Seminary. The map of 1827 shows him living on the Southampton Road near Rock Valley Road. He is said to have raised fourteen hundred bushels of rye in a single year, worth a dollar a bushel and a great deal of money then — so much rye, early historians said, that it led to the manufacture of whiskey, and the old map shows a distillery on Broad Brook in the Rock Valley section, right by the old school. His given name may stand in other records as Perez or Peris, which is one reason he is hard to find, and a caution against matching him to any similar name without proof. A 1929 survey placed his marble stone in the ninth row from the South Drive, marker 3. This man's grave has not been surveyed. The pin is provisional — it marks the burial ground, not the grave.", "sourceNote": "Service record, from Wyatt E. Harper, The Story of Holyoke (published for the centennial of the City of Holyoke, 1973) — full researched narrative pending. Harper names him among the Revolutionary graves in this burial ground; the grave itself has not yet been located on the ground.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_jones_ebenezer", "name": "Jones, Ebenezer", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "", "branchLabel": "Revolutionary War", "lat": 42.187206, "lng": -72.629402, "photo": "", "narrative": "[FIRST DRAFT] Ebenezer Jones enlisted August 12, 1777 in Captain Ephraim Chapin's company of Colonel Ruggles Woodbridge's regiment and was discharged October 23 — two months and seventeen days under General Gates with the Northern Army, including five days and a hundred miles walking home. Those are the dates of Saratoga. He was with Gates through the campaign that broke Burgoyne and brought France into the war, and then he walked back to this valley. The roll was sworn to in Hampshire County. He died April 26, 1839, aged 90. Chicopee historians write of an Ebenezer Jones who in 1753 was paid for a drum of his that had been used, in the words of the record, to be beat to call the People to meeting at proper seasons; when the drum finally broke the people used a conch shell instead. Whether that man was this man, or his father, has not been established. A 1929 survey placed his marble stone in the twenty-fifth row from the South Drive, marker 20. His service is taken from Sarah Rusk's 1980 record of Revolutionary patriots and their wives buried in Holyoke, reproduced by Canal Days. This man's grave has not been surveyed. The pin is provisional — it marks the burial ground, not the grave.", "sourceNote": "Service record, from Wyatt E. Harper, The Story of Holyoke (published for the centennial of the City of Holyoke, 1973) — full researched narrative pending. Harper names him among the Revolutionary graves in this burial ground; the grave itself has not yet been located on the ground.", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_miller_abner", "name": "Miller, Abner", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187237, "lng": -72.62941, "photo": "", "narrative": "Abner Miller (1755–1829) was a foundational pioneer of Ireland Parish whose familial ties and post-war enterprises were woven into the very infrastructure of early Holyoke. Born in Springfield on September 27, 1755, he was the son of Lieutenant John Miller Jr. and Hepsibah Chapin. Far from being an untraceable transient, his parentage placed him directly at the heart of the settlement's earliest multi-generational families. In the dangerous, volatile early decades of the frontier community, when the threat of raids still lingered after dark, the Millers were one of the six tight-knit local clans—alongside the Balls, Chapins, Days, Elys, and Morgans—who routinely \"forted together\" in shared homesteads for mutual nighttime defense. The familial networks traversing this corner of Elmwood Cemetery run incredibly deep. The Merab Miller who married fellow patriot Charles Ball (who rests just ten rows away) was not a stranger to Abner; she was his biological younger sister, born in 1761. Right after the conclusion of the war, Abner left a permanent mark on the physical geography of the Pioneer Valley. In 1785, he constructed a substantial homestead on Northampton Street that stood for nearly a century as a famous regional landmark. Operating for decades as the Abner Miller Tavern, the building served as a bustling, vital stagecoach stopover on the prominent travel route running between Springfield and Northampton, making Abner a widely known figure to travelers and locals alike. Though the specific company rosters, regiments, and precise muster dates of his Revolutionary War service remain obscured, his community standing as an honored Continental veteran is undisputed. Abner passed away in West Springfield (the rural parish territory that later became Holyoke) on June 24, 1829, at the age of 73. A 1929 cemetery survey accurately mapped his final resting place, positioning his simple, weathered marble stone in the eighth row from the South Drive at Marker 2, anchoring his memory forever to the historic soil he helped defend and build. Geolocation is pending. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "sourceNote": "", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_morgan_jesse", "name": "Morgan, Jesse", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Capt. Enoch Chapin's Co. of Minutemen, Col. John Moseley's Regiment", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187268, "lng": -72.629402, "photo": "", "narrative": "Jesse Morgan (1749–1810) was a rugged militia veteran whose dual commitments as a swift-reacting Minuteman and a long-campaign infantryman placed him at the heart of America's founding struggle. Born a twin to his brother Judah on March 22, 1749, in the rural west side of Springfield (the frontier territory that would later become West Springfield and eventually present-day Holyoke), he was the son of Joseph and Mary (Stebbins) Morgan. When a hard-riding courier tore through the Connecticut River Valley crying out the news of the bloody clashes at Lexington and Concord, 26-year-old Jesse dropped his farming tools and marched out on April 20, 1775. He served as a frontline Minuteman under Captain Enoch Chapin—a fellow celebrated leader from Ireland Parish. Though his initial response to the historic Lexington Alarm lasted just five frantic days before he returned to his fields, it firmly anchored him to the spark of the Revolution. As the war deepened, Jesse's service called him back to the ranks. In the autumn of 1777, as British General John Burgoyne's forces pressed southward from Canada, Jesse marched out with Captain Joseph Morgan's company under Colonel John Moseley's Hampshire County Regiment. Deployed from September 20 to October 17, 1777, Jesse and his comrades joined the vital expedition to the Northern Department, arriving in theater just in time to reinforce American positions during the decisive Saratoga Campaign. Following the monumental British surrender and a month and three days of tense maneuvering, his deployment concluded with a punishing, six-day, 120-mile tactical march back across the commonwealth entirely on foot to reach his home. While early local antiquarian accounts traditionally noted that \"very little information about Jesse Morgan\" survived, official Massachusetts records preserve a clear picture of his profound wartime sacrifice. Jesse passed away on June 16, 1810, at the age of 61, earning the solemn historical distinction of being the earliest recorded death among all Revolutionary War patriots buried at Elmwood Cemetery. A meticulous 1929 cemetery blueprint accurately documented his final resting place in the 14th row from the South Drive at Marker 13, positioned in the exact same row as his older brother and officer, Captain Joseph Morgan. His verified service remains preserved in local history through Sarah Rusk's 1980 compilation of local patriots, reproduced by Canal Days. Geolocation is pending; a provisional pin indicates the historic burying ground. The weathered marble stone stands as an enduring monument to a man who walked hundreds of miles to secure American independence. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "sourceNote": "", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_morgan_lucas", "name": "Morgan, Lucas", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Capt. Joseph Morgan's Co., Col. John Moseley's Regiment", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187294, "lng": -72.629378, "photo": "", "narrative": "Lucas Morgan was a steadfast patriot of Ireland Parish whose life on the Massachusetts frontier was defined by civic duty, leadership, and rugged wartime service. Born into a prominent regional pioneer family on February 26, 1743, he was the son of Joseph and Mary (Stebbins) Morgan. Long before the canals were cut, Lucas built a life along the western side of the Connecticut River on Northampton Street, marrying Elizabeth \"Betsey\" Eastman of Granby. When regional resistance crystallized into the Third Hampshire County Militia Regiment, Lucas answered the call, enlisting under the command of his older brother, Captain Joseph Morgan. His military record features two distinct chapters of service: the Ticonderoga Expedition to reinforce the Northern Army against British advances, and the Saratoga Campaign, as the British pressed south. This vital, month-long deployment saw the local militia bottleneck British forces. Following the monumental campaign, Lucas and his comrades faced a grueling, six-day, 120-mile trek back across the commonwealth entirely on foot. Local lore preserves a chilling, shadowy vignette tied directly to the old Lucas Morgan Homestead on Northampton Street—(later known to generations of Holyokers as the Fairfield Homestead). According to antiquarian records, a Morgan man returned to the farm on a bitter winter night to find his horse completely refusing to step into the stable. Reaching blindly into the dark to investigate, his hand brushed against a figure crouched silently in the shadows. He bolted for the house, throwing the heavy wooden door shut just as the thud of tomahawk blows began ringing through the oak. He fired a single musket shot blindly into the winter night, scattering the intruders. The next morning revealed a dead farm dog and the fresh tracks of three men trailing off through the deep snow. While early local accounts attempted to date this harrowing encounter to 1741 and attach it to Lucas, the timeline predates his birth. However, the physical reality of the legend endured for over a century; the heavy side door of the Northampton Street homestead proudly bore the distinct, deep scars of those historical tomahawk blows well into the modern era. Beyond his wartime achievements, Lucas helped lay the political foundation of the city. He served on the historic three-man committee alongside Lieutenant John Miller and Lieutenant Charles Ball that successfully petitioned the General Court to incorporate the independent boundaries of Ireland Parish, carving the emerging community of Holyoke out of West Springfield. Lucas lived to see the normalization of the young republic, passing away at the age of 74 on November 12, 1817. A blueprint survey of Revolutionary War graves documented his final resting place at Elmwood Cemetery, where his historic marble marker stands firm in the nineteenth row from the South Drive at marker 17—resting quietly alongside his courageous brothers, Joseph and Jesse. His gravesite is pending geolocation. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "sourceNote": "", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}, {"id": "harper_rev_morgan_titus", "name": "Morgan, Titus", "era": "Revolutionary War", "branch": "Capt. Jonathan Bardwell's Co., Col. Elisha Porter's Regiment", "branchLabel": "", "lat": 42.187311, "lng": -72.629342, "photo": "", "narrative": "Titus Morgan was born in West Springfield on July 17, 1740, to Joseph and Mary (Stebbins) Morgan. Long before the factories dominated the landscape, his family lines were inextricably tied to the valley. Answering the call of the infant republic, Titus enlisted on October 20, 1777, serving as a Private in Captain Jonathan Bardwell's company within Colonel Elisha Porter's 4th Hampshire County Regiment. He marched with the Northern Army for a grueling deployment lasting one month and two and a half days. His service placed him right in the middle of the rapid New England troop movements supporting the critical Saratoga campaign. When the war ended, Titus returned to the Pioneer Valley to reshape its physical geography. Alongside his eldest son, Erastus Morgan, he constructed a historic gristmill and sawmill on the westerly banks of the rapids above the site of the current Holyoke dam. Working the rugged banks, Titus and his son became the very first men to harness the massive water power of the Hadley Falls rapids—unleashing the force that everything the industrial city of Holyoke afterward was built upon. Their early timber enterprise laid a literal foundation for the local economy. The mill was eventually absorbed into the massive Connecticut Valley Lumber Company. Beyond his industrial legacy, Titus was the great-uncle of Junius Spencer Morgan and great-great-uncle to international financier J.P. Morgan. Titus lived to the remarkable age of 94, passing on November 28, 1834. A meticulous 1929 local survey positioned his weathered marble stone in the twenty-seventh row from the South Drive of Elmwood Cemetery, marked eternally as marker 21. His gravesite is pending geolocation. His frontline service remains preserved in local memory through Sarah Rusk's 1980 registry of Revolutionary patriots. If you have further information about this veteran, please contact the City of Holyoke Veterans Graves Officer at (413) 322-5630 at 310 Appleton Street, 1st Floor, Holyoke, MA 01040.", "sourceNote": "", "badges": [], "status": "located", "source": "harper-revolutionary-graves", "geo": "provisional"}]};
